% When trained on the optimal trajectories on a particular scene, the reverse diffusion process can learn to generate smooth, kinematically feasible trajectories between a start and a goal location during inference. The learned models also obtain an implicit understanding of collision avoidance in the scene~\cite{janner2022planning}. To further improve the performance and also generalization to changes in the scene, \textcolor{green}{the model can be trained on a large set of trajectories optimal for different scenes} and the learned reverse diffusion model can be perturbed by gradients from a cost function. In works like ~\cite{janner2022planning}, this is known as classifier-based guidance. Alg.\ref{algo1} shows the guided diffusion-based planning along the lines of~\cite{janner2022planning}, wherein the main gradient-based perturbation of the prior happens in line 2.

